text:
distinguish solids, liquids and gases using compressibility, rigidity, viscosity and the observation timescale;
explain why electromagnetic interactions dominate ordinary interatomic bonding;
relate interatomic force to potential energy, and obtain equilibrium separation and a small-displacement spring constant;
differentiate and interpret the Lennard–Jones potential;
relate a molar latent heat approximately to the energy of an individual intermolecular bond;
describe dispersion, ionic, covalent and metallic bonding, including their different physical origins;
explain the Madelung constant and construct a simple ionic-crystal energy model.

describe the assumptions of the ideal-gas model and use the equation of state;
explain thermal equilibrium, the zeroth law and the operational meaning of temperature;
distinguish the Celsius and kelvin scales and describe a constant-volume gas thermometer;
relate pressure, volume and temperature in simple transformations;
explain the statistical meaning of temperature and the principle of equipartition;
calculate internal energy and molar heat capacities of ideal gases, including the effect of molecular degrees of freedom.

derive the pressure of a dilute gas from elastic molecular collisions with a wall;
distinguish velocity (a signed vector component) from speed (a non-negative magnitude);
explain the Boltzmann factor and the physical meaning of the Maxwell–Boltzmann distributions;
derive the one-dimensional Maxwell–Boltzmann velocity distribution using Gaussian normalisation;
derive the two- and three-dimensional speed distributions using circular annuli and spherical shells in velocity space;
recognise that a more rigorous treatment of continuous probability and velocity-space geometry is given in the appendix;
calculate the mean, most probable and root-mean-square speeds, and connect the distributions to average kinetic energy.
